    52	\subsection{Correctness}\label{sec:correctness}
    53	This section is devoted to the proof of the correctness of the reduction, as stated in the following lemma.
    54	
    55	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:corrrect-w1}
    56		If the input $G,k$ is a positive instance for the independent set problem if and only if $(H,S,D)$ is a positive \isdts instance.
    57	\end{lemma}
    58	
    59	We now prove the first implication. Assume that $X=\{v_1,\ldots,v_k\}\subseteq V(G)$ is an independent set of $G$ of size $k$. We move the tokens in $H$ as follows.
    60	\begin{enumerate}
    61		\item For every $i\le k$, move the token from $s_{3,i}$ to $c_{i,v_i}$. Similarly, move tokens from $s_{1,i}$ and $s_{2,i}$ to $a_{i,v_i}$, and $b_{i,v_i}$ respectively.
    62		\item Move the token from $x$ to $x'$ and the token from $y$ to $y'$.
    63		\item For every $i\le k$, move the token from $a_{i,v_i}$ to $q_{i,v_i}$. And then from $q_{i,v_i}$ to $d_{1,i}$.
    64		\item Move the token from $z$ to $z'$.
    65		\item For every $i\le k$, move the token from $b_{i,v_i}$ to $d_{2,i}$ and the tokens from $c_{i,v_i}$ to $d_{3,i}$.
    66	\end{enumerate}
    67	
    68	The first set of moves is possible because a token on $c_{i,v_i}$ blocks in $A_i$ every $a_{i,u}$ with $u\neq v_i$, but $a_{i,v_i}$ is free. The same holds for $b_{i,v_i}$.
    69	%
    70	Second, the clocks $x,y$ can move to $x',y'$ because $S_1$, $S_2$, and $S_3$, are free of token, freeing $D_3$.
    71	%
    72	Third, $X$ is an independent set in $G$, and therefore $v_i\in X$ is not adjacent to any $v_j \in X$ for $j\neq i$. Therefore, $q_{i,v_i}$ is not adjacent to any $b_{j,v_j}$ for $j\neq i$. Hence, the tokens can move from $A$ to $Q$ and then to $D_1$.
    73	%
    74	Forth, as~$Q$ is empty of token, $z$ can move to $z'$, freeing $D_2$.
    75	%
    76	Last, the tokens in $B$ and $C$ can freely move to $D_2$ and $D_3$ respectively.
    77	
    78	This shows that $(H,S,D)$ is a positive \isdts instance, and conclude the first part of the proof of \cref{lem:corrrect-w1}.
    79	%
    80	The second implication in the statement of \cref{lem:corrrect-w1} is harder to prove. We show that any reconfiguration sequence must follow some structure, from which we derive the existence of an independent set in $G$. In what follows, we assume that $(H,S,D)$ is a positive \isdts instance. 
    81	
    82	\begin{claim}
    83		For any reconfiguration sequence, the tokens in $x$, $y$, and $z$ end up in $x'$, $y'$, and $z'$ respectively. Furthermore, they move in this order. 
    84	\end{claim}
    85	\begin{claimproof}
    86		While other tokens can reach $x'$, the token on $x$ can only reach $x'$. Also, the token in~$y$ (resp. $z$) is the only one that can reach $y'$ (resp. $z'$).
    87	
    88		Additionally, notice that both $y$ and $z'$ block all of $Q$. As the token reaching $D_1$ need to go through $Q$, if $z$ moves to $z'$ before $y$ moves to $y'$ no token can ever reach $Q$. So $y$ must reach $y'$ before $z$ reaches $z'$. And since $x$ blocks $y'$, $x$ must slide to $x'$ first.
    89	\end{claimproof}
    90	
    91	\begin{claim}
    92		\label{claim:tightly_controlled_paths}
    93		For any reconfiguration sequence, no token slides along the edge $uv$, if $uv$ is in $A\times C$, $B \times C$ or $B\times Q$.
    94	\end{claim}
    95	\begin{claimproof}
    96		As the tokes coming from the clocks stay within them, we may argue as if these vertices and their respective tokens were not present.
    97		
    98		The vertices in $D_2$ can only be reached from $S_2$, thus no token may leave a path from some vertex in $S_2$ to some vertex in $D_2$ which prohibits the use of edges between $B$ and $C$ or $B$ and $Q$. Consequently and similarly, the only tokens that can end up in $D_1$ are these that start in $S_1$, prohibiting the use of the edges between $A$ and $C$. 
