   100	
   101	\begin{claim}
   102		There exists a set $X=\{v_1,\ldots v_k\}\subseteq V(G)$ such that when $y$ moves to $y'$, there are tokens on vertices $a_{i,v_i}$, $b_{i,v_i}$, and $c_{i,v_i}$ for every $i\le k$.
   103	\end{claim}
   104	\begin{claimproof}
   105		When $y$ moves to $y'$, $x$ already moved to $x'$, so $S_1$, $S_2$, and $S_3$ are empty of tokens. Furthermore, as $y$ and $z$ did not move yet, $D_2$, $D_3$, $Q$ and therefore $D_1$ are empty of token. So the tokens in $S_3$ moved to $C_3$. We then define $v_i$ as the vertex of $V(G)$ such that $s_{3,i}$ moved to $c_{i,v_i}$. Finally, note that $c_{i,v_i}$ blocks in $A_i$ every $a_{i,u}$ with $u\neq v_i$. So the token from $s_{1,i}$ must now be in $a_{i,v_i}$, as these tokens may not move to other blocs by \cref{claim:tightly_controlled_paths}.  Similarly, the token from $s_{2,i}$ must now be in $b_{i,v_i}$.
